Spring Kafka's `DefaultErrorHandler` (the modern replacement for `SeekToCurrentErrorHandler`) handles listener exceptions: retry the failing record with backoff, then publish to a Dead Letter Topic if all retries fail.

```java
@Configuration
@RequiredArgsConstructor
public class KafkaErrorHandlingConfig {

    private final KafkaTemplate<String, Object> template;

    @Bean
    public DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler() {
        // Publish to <topic>.DLT with the failure metadata in headers
        DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer recoverer = new DeadLetterPublishingRecoverer(template,
            (rec, ex) -> new TopicPartition(rec.topic() + ".DLT", rec.partition()));

        // Exponential backoff: 1s, 2s, 4s, 8s, 16s — total 5 retries (~31s)
        ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries backOff = new ExponentialBackOffWithMaxRetries(5);
        backOff.setInitialInterval(1_000L);
        backOff.setMultiplier(2.0);
        backOff.setMaxInterval(16_000L);

        DefaultErrorHandler handler = new DefaultErrorHandler(recoverer, backOff);

        // Non-retryable exceptions → straight to DLT, no retry
        handler.addNotRetryableExceptions(
            DeserializationException.class,
            MessageConversionException.class,
            ValidationException.class);

        // Observability hooks
        handler.setRetryListeners((record, ex, deliveryAttempt) ->
            log.warn("Retry attempt {} for {} : {}",
                deliveryAttempt, record.topic(), ex.getMessage()));

        return handler;
    }

    @Bean
    public ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, Object> kafkaListenerContainerFactory(
            ConsumerFactory<String, Object> cf, DefaultErrorHandler errorHandler) {
        ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<String, Object> f = new ConcurrentKafkaListenerContainerFactory<>();
        f.setConsumerFactory(cf);
        f.setCommonErrorHandler(errorHandler);
        return f;
    }
}

@Component
public class OrderListener {
    @KafkaListener(topics = "orders", containerFactory = "kafkaListenerContainerFactory")
    public void onOrder(OrderEvent ev) {
        try { processor.process(ev); }
        catch (TransientException e) { throw e; }            // → retry
        catch (PoisonException e)   { throw new NotRetryableException(e); }  // → DLT immediately
    }
}

// DLT consumer for monitoring / manual replay
@KafkaListener(topics = "orders.DLT", groupId = "orders-dlt-monitor")
public void onDeadLetter(OrderEvent ev,
                         @Header(KafkaHeaders.DLT_EXCEPTION_MESSAGE) String reason,
                         @Header(KafkaHeaders.DLT_ORIGINAL_TOPIC)    String origTopic,
                         @Header(KafkaHeaders.DLT_EXCEPTION_STACKTRACE) String trace) {
    alerts.send("DLT received from " + origTopic + ": " + reason);
}
```

- **Why "seek to current"?** On retry, the consumer seeks back to the failing record's offset and re-polls — no offset commit advances until the record either succeeds or is recovered to DLT. Other records in the same poll batch are paused and replayed on success.
- **Backoff matters** — fixed 100 ms retries on a downstream outage spam the failing service. Use exponential backoff with a cap. Five attempts over ~30 seconds is a sane default.
- **Mark non-retryable exceptions** (deserialization, validation, business invariant) so they skip retry and go straight to DLT — re-running them will never succeed and just delays processing of healthy messages.
- The DLT carries forensic headers (`X-Original-Topic`, `X-Exception-Message`, `X-Exception-Stacktrace`). Build a small DLT viewer to triage and a replay tool to resend after fixing the root cause.
- **Container-level** vs **listener-level** error handlers: `setCommonErrorHandler` on the factory applies to all listeners; per-listener override via `@RetryableTopic` for finer control.
